 Theorem: An acute triangle has a largest angle of less than $\theta = 90^\circ;$ an obtuse triangle has a largest angle of greater than  $\theta = 90^\circ.$  Because a triangle cannot have two right angles, if the largest angle of a triangle is greater than 90 degrees, then no two sides have lengths that add up to the length of the third side.  Therefore, an obtuse triangle will have at least two sides with lengths that add up to greater than the length of the third side.  The following vertices in the coordinate plane are translated to an octagon with sides from the origin $O.$

[asy]
