 Equation both sides with $x + 8 = k \sqrt{x - 1},$ where $k$ is a positive constant.  Squaring both sides, we get
\[\frac{(x + 8)^2}{x - 1} = k^2 (x - 1).\]This simplifies to the equation
\begin{align*}
k^2 x^2 - (2k^2 + 17k + 65) x + 9k^2 + 32 &= 0 \\
&= (x - 1)(kx + 9k + 32) \\
&= 0.
\end{align*}For this equation to hold for all $x > 1,$ the constant $k$ must satisfy
\[9k + 32 = 0,\]which gives us $k = -\frac{32}{9}.$

Therefore, we have
\[\frac{x + 8}{\sqrt{x - 1}} = \sqrt{\left( -\frac{32}{9} \right)^2 x^2 - \left( 2 \left( -\frac{32}{9} \right)^2 + 17 \left( -\frac{32}{9} \right) + 65 \right) x + 9 \left( -\frac{32}{9} \right)^2 + 32}.\]The constant term is always positive, so the range of this expression is $\boxed{[9, \infty)},$ as shown below (graph of the function; dashed line is $y = x,$ plotted for reference).

